<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Camino Real (California)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Camino Real ('the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a>') was the route linking the 21 Spanish missions of Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, together with its presidios and pueblos, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> to Sonoma. It developed from 1769 as Franciscan missionaries and soldiers moved north along the coast. Parts of its course are followed by modern highways and marked with mission-bell signposts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-camino-real-california.jpg" alt="El Camino Real (California)" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ramalama_22 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Road_to_LA_-_El_Camino_Real_Bell_in_Front_of_San_Gabriel_Mission_(5540781815).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
